{
  "requirement": "`kind` classifies the construct and `span` locates it under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span`. Language providers can\nconstruct an attachment from these two fields.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OutlineAttachment {\n    pub kind: AttachmentKind,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\nimpl OutlineAttachment {\n    pub fn new(kind: AttachmentKind, span: Span) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OutlineAttachment;\nimpl Debug for OutlineAttachment;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.sdk.outlines.OutlineAttachment`:**\n1. The type has public `kind: AttachmentKind` and `span: Span` fields, and is `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n2. `OutlineAttachment::new(kind, span)` builds an attachment from those two fields.\n3. The type implements `Clone` and `Debug`.\n4. `span` locates the construct as the cited `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span` describes. That requirement is context only, so I checked that the tests apply it here and did not reassess it.\n\n**Coverage:**\n- **Constructor and fields.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` has a custom language whose `outline` calls `OutlineAttachment::new(AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\".into()), *span)`.\n  - The test loads the snapshot and asserts `attachment.kind == AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\")`. It also asserts that `&snapshot.text()[attachment.span] == \"é\"`. Together these show `new` preserves both fields and that providers can build attachments from the two fields.\n  - `fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` also uses `OutlineAttachment::new` with `Comment` and `Annotation` kinds.\n- **Span semantics in the subject's case.** The same test checks:\n  - A span that is valid and covers the multibyte `é` slices to exactly `é`.\n  - Spans that are out of bounds, split a character, extend past the item, or are misordered are rejected.\n  - Two spans are accepted when they are in order, and the same spans are rejected when out of order.\n  \n  These cover the half-open byte-range and original-snapshot behavior that the subject cites.\n- **Declaration shape and trait impls.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` generates rustdoc and compares the declaration, the inherent members and the trait implementations against the requirement's listings.\n  - Through `check_declaration` and `trait_impls`, it asserts that the documented `Clone` and `Debug` impls equal the requirement's list.\n  - It also checks the struct and `new` signature. It additionally compiles the shipping consumer against the SDK.\n\nI found no obligation without a test and no assertion that contradicts the requirement. There are no acknowledgments.",
